// BIBLE REFERENCE: Mark 11:1-7; Luke 19:30-36; Matthew 21:1-9
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Zechariah 9:9
//     Gospel: Matthew 21:4-5; John 12:14-15
//     Insight: Peter witnessed the prophetic fulfillment firsthand,
//     as one of the disciples sent to retrieve the donkey colt.
//   - Isaiah 62:11
//     Gospel: Matthew 21:5
//     Insight: "Behold, the Lord has proclaimed to Zion, 'See, your
//     salvation comes!'" The donkey retrieval was part of the
//     divine preparation for the king's entry.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

-> start

=== start ===
The morning dawned differently from any other. The Teacher's voice
carried an urgency I had never heard. "Go into the village ahead of
you," He told James and John and me. "You will find a colt tied
near the gate — a young donkey that has never been ridden. Untie it
and bring it to Me. If anyone asks, say, 'The Lord needs it.'"

* [The colt was prepared?] -> prepared_colt
* [Why the specific words?] -> specific_words
* [The owner recognized you.] -> owner_recognized

=== prepared_colt ===
I have tended animals since childhood — my nets were not the only
thing I knew how to handle. This colt was exceptional. Its coat shone
like polished chestnut in the morning light. Its ears pricked at every
sound. It was not skittish — it was... waiting. As if it knew.

* [The colt knew what was coming.] -> knowing_animal
* [Never ridden before.] -> never_ridden_peter

=== specific_words ===
"The Lord needs it." James thought I was mad when the Teacher
gave us those exact words. But He knew — He knew the owner
would recognize the phrase. Tobias, the man who kept the colt,
he had been expecting this moment for decades. His grandfather
had owned the animal's sire. Three generations of preparation.

* [Prophecy prepared.] -> generational_preparation
* [The phrase was a key.] -> phrase_key

=== owner_recognized ===
Tobias looked at us, then at the colt, then at the road ahead.
His eyes were bright with unshed tears. "The Lord needs it,"
he repeated. As if to himself. As if confirming what he already
knew. Then he handed us the reins without another word.

* [He had been waiting.] -> prophetic_waiting
* [No charge was mentioned.] -> no_charge

=== knowing_animal ===
Animals know when something is holy. This colt did not
resist. It stepped forward as we approached. It allowed
the garments to be laid upon it without hesitation. It
walked with purpose down the road — head high, steps
measured. Even the disciples who had trained with us
in handling beasts of burden were surprised by its
composure.

* [Peace before power.] -> peace_power
* [The colt cooperated.] -> colt_cooperated

=== never_ridden_peter ===
In our law, an animal that has never been ridden is set
apart for sacred work. To ride it is to claim a holy
moment. The Teacher knew this. He was not simply
borrowing transportation. He was claiming His
messianic identity through the most precise
fulfillment of scripture possible.

* [More than transportation.] -> more_than_transport
* [Sacred symbolism.] -> sacred_symbolism

=== generational_preparation ===
Tobias told us — his voice shaking slightly — that
his family had kept this line of donkeys for over
a century. Each generation prepared for the moment
when the king would come. They never knew exactly
when. But they kept the colts unridden, just in case.

* [Centuries of waiting.] -> centuries_waiting
* [Faith across generations.] -> faith_generations

=== phrase_key ===
"The Lord needs it" — James called it the password
of prophecy. The owner's face changed when he heard
it. Relief. Recognition. Surrender. He did not ask
for payment. He did not ask for questions. He simply
stepped aside and let the moment unfold.

* [The phrase unlocked more than the colt.] -> unlocked_moment
* [A password of faith.] -> password_faith

=== prophetic_waiting ===
His grandfather had told him the story — Zechariah's
prophecy, written five centuries before the Teacher
was born. "Your king comes to you... humble and
riding on a donkey." Tobias had waited his whole
life for someone to ask. When we came, he ran to
meet us.

* [Waiting for a promise.] -> waiting_promise
* [Faith rewarded.] -> faith_rewarded

=== no_charge ===
No charge, no contract, no receipt. Just an old
man placing a precious colt into the hands of
Galilean fishermen. The crowd that gathered
saw it happen. They understood. A king who
enters without cost will reign without price.

* [The entry was free.] -> entry_free
* [Grace before glory.] -> grace_before

=== peace_power ===
The colt walked peacefully. Not in fear. Not in
confusion. But with the dignity of one who
knew its purpose. It was the perfect symbol —
power restrained. Strength submitted. Authority
expressed through gentleness.

* [Gentleness as strength.] -> gentleness_strength
* [Power under control.] -> power_control

=== colt_cooperated ===
The colt did not need to be forced. It did not
need to be driven. When we placed the garments
on its back, it lowered itself naturally to
receive them. As if it understood that what
was about to happen was greater than
transportation. Greater than ceremony.

* [Surrender to sacred purpose.] -> sacred_surrender
* [The animal knew holiness.] -> animal_holiness

=== more_than_transport ===
The Teacher did not need the colt for mobility.
He needed it for meaning. Every detail of
prophecy was being fulfilled: the unridden
beast, the humble mount, the cloaks as
saddle. This was not convenience. This was
consecration.

* [Every detail symbolic.] -> symbolic_detail
* [Meaning over means.] -> meaning_means

=== sacred_symbolism ===
The donkey of peace. The colt of humility.
The unridden beast of sacred purpose. All
of Israel knew what these symbols meant.
A king who comes on a donkey comes
not to conquer but to reconcile.
Not to destroy but to redeem.

* [Reconciliation over conquest.] -> reconcile_conquer
* [The symbols spoke clearly.] -> symbols_spoke

=== centuries_waiting ===
Five hundred years of waiting. Five centuries
of promises written in the margins of scrolls.
Five centuries of elders telling children,
"Someday, a king will come like this."
And on this morning, the someday arrived.

* [The waiting was worth it.] -> waiting_worth
* [Promises kept.] -> promises_kept

=== faith_generations ===
Each generation that kept the colts unridden
was making a statement: we believe the
prophecy. We are preparing. We are
waiting. And when the stranger came
asking for a colt, they recognized
the fulfillment.

* [Faith preserved.] -> faith_preserved
* [Waiting rewarded.] -> waiting_rewards

=== unlocked_moment ===
The phrase unlocked more than
the colt. It unlocked Tobias's
heart. It unlocked the crowd's
recognition. It unlocked the
fulfillment of prophecy. "The
Lord needs it" — and suddenly
everything made sense.

* [Preparation completed.] -> preparation_done
* [The moment revealed.] -> moment_revealed

=== password_faith ===
"The Lord needs it" — the
password of faith. Spoken
by disciples. Recognized by
the owner. Verified by
prophecy. And the colt
stepped forward to
serve its king.

* [Faith spoken in words.] -> faith_words
* [The password worked.] -> password_worked

=== peace_power ===
As we made our way down
the road, the colt moving
peacefully beneath us,
I understood: the Teacher
was not just entering
Jerusalem. He was
entering history.
And we were part
of the story.

* [Part of something bigger.] -> bigger_story
* [History in motion.] -> history_motion

=== entry_free ===
No price was paid.
No contract was signed.
No tax was collected.
The king entered
freely, because the
people recognized
that what was given
freely is worth
more than
anything that
can be purchased.

* [Freedom as gift.] -> freedom_gift
* [Grace before glory.] -> grace_glory_peter

=== grace_before ===
We entered Jerusalem
not with the pomp of
kings, but with the
grace of servants.
The Teacher had
washed our feet
just days before.
Now He rode on
a beast that
had never been
ridden — because
His kingdom
does not
ride on
force.

* [Grace over power.] -> grace_over_peter
* [The kingdom arrives.] -> kingdom_arrives_peter

=== gentleness_strength ===
The colt was gentle.
The Teacher was
gentle. But there
was power in that
gentleness. The
power to
transform. The
power to
heal. The power
to save.
Gentleness was
not weakness.
It was
authority
perfectly
exercised.

* [Perfect authority.] -> perfect_authority
* [Strength in gentleness.] -> strength_gentle

=== power_control ===
The power was
controlled. The
strength was
channeled. The
authority was
exercised
through
gentleness and
peace. The
king on the
donkey did
not
threaten
Rome.
He
challenged
hearts.

* [Heart over empire.] -> heart_empire
* [Authority perfected.] -> authority_perfect

=== symbolic_detail ===
Every detail was
symbolic. The
colt. The
garments. The
road. The
crowd. The
timing. All
of it worked
together
to declare:
the king
has come.
in
peace.
in
truth.
in
love.

* [The symbol speaks.] -> symbol_speaks
* [Details matter.] -> details_matter_peter

=== meaning_means ===
The meaning
was not
in the
means.
The means
were
the message.
The donkey
was not
transportation.
It was
testimony.
The colt
was not
a tool.
It was
a
testament.
To the
kingdom
that
was
coming.

* [Testimony in motion.] -> testimony_motion
* [The kingdom declared.] -> kingdom_declared

=== reconcile_conquer ===
The king who
conquers by
reconciliation.
Who defeats
by
forgiveness.
Who wins
by
loving
his
enemies.
The
donkey
was not
a
military
steed.
It was
a
statement
of peace.
Of
reconciliation.
Of
a
different
kind
of
victory.

-> DONE

=== closing_peter ===
Now I understand
why the Teacher
wept over Jerusalem.
Not because of
what it would
face. But
because of what
it had
received.
The king had
come.
In peace.
In truth.
In love.
And
Jerusalem
would
never
be
the
same.

-> DONE